-- =============================================================================
-- Type errors in multi-clause DECIDE groups are reported at the clause that
-- has them, in terms of the clauses (review of legalese/l4-ide#545, items 3
-- and 4). The warnings that go with them are pinned in
-- jl4-core/test/PatternClausesMissingSpec.hs.
-- =============================================================================

-- (3) The clauses after one that matches every input are never used, and
-- they are still checked: the third clause's body is a NUMBER where a STRING
-- is due, and the fourth reads a name that does not exist.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A STRING
DECIDE `shadowed clauses` 0 IS "zero"
DECIDE `shadowed clauses` n IS "other"
DECIDE `shadowed clauses` 1 IS 42
DECIDE `shadowed clauses` 2 IS `no such thing`

-- (4) A body of the wrong type in a later clause is reported at that body.
GIVEN m IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `wrong body` (JUST x) IS x
DECIDE `wrong body` NOTHING  IS "none"

-- (4) A pattern of the wrong type is reported as one for the input it
-- stands for ...
GIVEN m IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `wrong pattern` (JUST x) IS 1
DECIDE `wrong pattern` TRUE     IS 2

-- ... also when there is no GIVEN to name the input.
DECIDE `wrong pattern without given` (JUST 1) IS 1
DECIDE `wrong pattern without given` TRUE     IS 2

-- (4) With no GIVETH, the clauses are read from the top, so the first clause
-- settles the result type and the clause that disagrees is the one reported:
-- "three", not the 1 and 2 above it.
DECLARE Colour IS ONE OF Red, Green, Blue

DECIDE `no giveth` Red   IS 1
DECIDE `no giveth` Green IS 2
DECIDE `no giveth` Blue  IS "three"

-- ... and likewise for patterns: TRUE is reported, not the two clauses that
-- agree on a MAYBE.
DECIDE `odd last pattern` (JUST 1) IS 1
DECIDE `odd last pattern` NOTHING  IS 2
DECIDE `odd last pattern` TRUE     IS 3

-- (3) The clauses after one that matches every input are checked, then
-- dropped, so the clause before them cannot see their binding: a body that
-- names it is reported as an unknown name here, rather than passing the check
-- and failing when it runs.
GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `refs dead` c   IS `__pm_fallthrough_0`
DECIDE `refs dead` Red IS 2

GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `refs mid` Red   IS 1
DECIDE `refs mid` c     IS `__pm_fallthrough_1`
DECIDE `refs mid` Green IS 3
